The OTLP exporter package handles **all three signals** (traces, metrics, logs). Skip the `OpenTelemetry` package alone — `OpenTelemetry.Extensions.Hosting` is what wires up DI.

## Complete Program.cs

```csharp
using OpenTelemetry.Resources;
using OpenTelemetry.Trace;
using OpenTelemetry.Metrics;
using OpenTelemetry.Logs;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry()
    .ConfigureResource(resource => resource
        .AddService(serviceName: builder.Environment.ApplicationName))
    // ---- TRACES ----
    .WithTracing(tracing => tracing
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation(options =>
        {
            // Keep health checks out of your traces
            options.Filter = httpContext =>
                !httpContext.Request.Path.StartsWithSegments("/healthz");
        })
        .AddHttpClientInstrumentation(options =>
        {
            options.RecordException = true;
        }))
    // ---- METRICS ----
    .WithMetrics(metrics => metrics
        .AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation()
        .AddHttpClientInstrumentation())
    // ---- LOGS ----
    .WithLogging(logging =>
    {
        logging.IncludeScopes = true;
    })
    // Single OTLP exporter for traces, metrics, AND logs.
    // Reads OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT (defaults to http://localhost:4317, gRPC).
    .UseOtlpExporter();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/", () => "Hello World!");

app.Run();
```

